# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit the real .env (see .gitignore).
#
# SEC-006 — DOS COSAS QUE CAMBIARON Y QUE IMPORTAN ANTES DE EDITAR NADA:
#
# 1. ESTE FICHERO NO SE LEÍA. `docker compose` busca su fichero de interpolación en el directorio del
#    PROYECTO (deploy/compose/), no en la raíz del repositorio, donde está este fichero. Así que los
#    16 `${VAR:-default}` del compose se resolvían sin leerlo nunca. El Makefile pasa ahora
#    `--env-file .env`. Si invocas `docker compose` a mano, pásalo tú — sin él, poner
#    AEON_OTEL_ENDPOINT aquí no apunta el stack a ningún sitio, y poner AEON_CALLER_TOKEN aquí no
#    rota la credencial del worker. Las dos cosas están medidas en docs/secrets.md.
#
# 2. TODA CREDENCIAL DE AQUÍ ACEPTA `<NOMBRE>_FILE`, y esa es la ruta para claves que no son tuyas:
#    el valor nunca entra en el entorno del proceso, así que no está en `docker inspect`, ni en
#    /proc/<pid>/environ, ni en el entorno de los procesos hijos (el worker lanza la CLI `claude`, que
#    hereda os.environ entero). `make dev-secret-files` + `make dev-secrets`. Ver docs/secrets.md.
#
#    Poner las DOS —`X` y `X_FILE`— hace que el servicio se niegue a arrancar, a propósito: dos
#    fuentes para un secreto significa que una rotación puede aplicarse a la que pierde sin que falle
#    nada.

# --- Cloud model providers (Model Gateway adapters, see roadmap §2.4 / MDL-003..005) ---
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
GOOGLE_API_KEY=

# --- Local inference via the Prometheus platform (MDL-006). Real OAuth2 client_credentials
# against a separate auth-service, not an API key — see docs/adr/0004 (resolved) and
# go/internal/providers/prometheus_inference/. Credentials come from `POST /admin/clients` on the
# auth-service; the client_secret is shown once and cannot be retrieved again.
PROMETHEUS_AUTH_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9000
PROMETHEUS_GATEWAY_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8020
PROMETHEUS_CLIENT_ID=
PROMETHEUS_CLIENT_SECRET=
# LEAVE THIS EMPTY unless you must pin the scope by hand (MDL-015). Empty means aeon-modelgw derives
# it from the ModelPolicyBundle, naming every prometheus_inference model the bundle declares.
#
# Setting it is how a real bug hid: this was a hand-written string naming ONE model, while routing
# picks the model per request, so every other candidate in the bundle came back
# 403 "This client is not authorized to use model '<id>'" — which made the bundle's declared fallback
# chain unusable in production. A hand-written list also goes stale silently the moment someone adds
# a candidate.
#
# Still confirmed live and still worth knowing: being AUTHORIZED for a model does not make the token
# include it — a token requested without the model:<id> scope comes back valid but
# GET /v1/models/mine shows zero models. And one token can carry several model scopes at once
# (measured: "inference:read model:gpt-oss-20b-mxfp4 model:qwen3-0.6b" is granted verbatim), which is
# why one token covers every candidate instead of one per call.
PROMETHEUS_SCOPE=
PROMETHEUS_DEFAULT_MODEL=

# --- Generic openai_compatible fallback (MDL-007), e.g. the dev vllm/ollama service ---
# No trailing /v1 — the adapter (go/internal/providers/openai_compatible) appends
# /v1/chat/completions itself; a trailing /v1 here would double it up.
OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000
OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_API_KEY=not-needed

AEON_TENANT_ID=default

# --- Memory Store provenance (MEM-001) ---
# HMAC key MemoryStore uses to compute provenance_hmac on every write, so tampering outside the
# store is detectable later (VerifyProvenance). docker-compose.yml falls back to an insecure
# dev-only default if this is unset — set a real, random value here for anything beyond local dev.
#
# SETTING THIS HERE DID NOTHING UNTIL SEC-006, and it is the worst place for that to have been true.
# compose's `environment:` block has precedence over `env_file:`, and its interpolated default was
# resolved without reading this file at all — measured: the value stayed
# `dev-only-insecure-memory-hmac-key-change-me`, which is committed and therefore public. Anyone who
# knows it can forge a provenance_hmac, which is the single thing MEM-001/SEC-004's tamper detection
# rests on. Fixed by the --env-file above; verify with
# `docker compose --env-file .env -f deploy/compose/docker-compose.yml config | grep HMAC`.
AEON_MEMORY_HMAC_KEY=

# --- Secret Broker (SEC-002, aeon-toolgw) ---
# Comma-separated list of secret names the broker loads at startup, one per AEON_SECRET_<NAME> env
# var below (dots become underscores, name is upper-cased — e.g. "demo" -> AEON_SECRET_DEMO). A
# lease for a name with no configured value simply fails (POST /secrets/issue returns 404) rather
# than this process refusing to start. Empty/unset until a real tool actually needs a credential —
# see backlog.md.
AEON_SECRET_NAMES=
# AEON_SECRET_DEMO=

# --- ABOM signing (FND-002, `aeon publish`) ---
# Hex-encoded 32-byte Ed25519 seed. Unset: `aeon publish` still signs and writes a real,
# self-verifying ABOM, but with a freshly generated key each run (a clear "ephemeral" warning is
# printed) — the same manifest published twice will NOT produce a byte-identical signature. Set a
# real, persisted value here for anything beyond local one-off publishing.
AEON_ABOM_SIGNING_KEY=

# --- Quality-aware routing (MDL-002, aeon-modelgw) ---
# A candidate whose most recently reported eval score (POST /quality-scores) is below this is
# skipped by routing entirely — never even attempted. Default (unset): 0.5.
AEON_QUALITY_SCORE_THRESHOLD=

# --- Web search (TOOL-007, aeon-toolgw) ---
# The SearXNG instance search.web queries. deploy/compose runs one, so the default value below is
# what the reference stack uses and needs no editing.
#
# Unset is a real, supported state and not a degraded one: search.web is then NOT registered at all,
# and a call to it fails with `unknown tool` on the first attempt. That is deliberate — it replaced a
# stub that answered {"status": "executed"} and searched nothing, which made a deployment with no
# search provider indistinguishable from a working one.
#
# Pointing this at a PUBLIC SearXNG instance changes the egress story: the query is written by the
# model from the run's context, so it can carry fragments of whatever the run is reading, and a
# public instance is a third party. The adapter declares itself in-network (recorded in every
# answer's `in_network` field); if you point it outside, that declaration becomes wrong.
AEON_WEBSEARCH_SEARXNG_URL=http://searxng:8080

# Host port the compose SearXNG publishes, for querying it by hand. Nothing in Aeon uses it.
# Overridable because 8080 is the most contended port on a developer machine.
AEON_SEARXNG_PORT=18080

# ---- Observabilidad: Argus (OBS-006b) --------------------------------------------------------
#
# El lado Python corre el SDK de Argus (`argus-obs-sdk`), así que lee SUS variables. El lado Go no
# tiene SDK de Argus, así que lee `AEON_OTEL_ENDPOINT` y emite los mismos atributos a mano.
#
# Apuntar TODO el stack a un agente de Argus son estas tres:
#
#   AEON_OTEL_ENDPOINT=host.docker.internal:4318          # los cuatro binarios Go
#   ARGUS_ENDPOINT=http://host.docker.internal:4318       # el worker Python
#   ARGUS_PROTOCOL=http/protobuf                          # ver abajo: NO es opcional
#
# CUIDADO 1 — `host.docker.internal`, no `localhost`. La convención de Argus es que las aplicaciones
# exporten a localhost, donde escucha su agente por máquina. Pero el agente se ata a `127.0.0.1`, así
# que desde un contenedor `localhost:4318` es inalcanzable — medido: curl devuelve 000 contra 200. Y
# el fallo es SILENCIOSO: el exportador reintenta de fondo, la app funciona, y ningún span llega.
#
# CUIDADO 2 — fijar `ARGUS_PROTOCOL`. Su Config autodetecta el protocolo por los paquetes
# instalados, y esta imagen tiene el exportador HTTP y el gRPC. Eligió gRPC, habló gRPC contra el
# puerto 4318 —que es HTTP— y todo falló con `StatusCode.UNAVAILABLE` en bucle de reintentos. Un
# protocolo autodetectado junto a un puerto configurado a mano pueden discrepar, y discrepan en
# ejecución, no al arrancar.
#
# Sin poner nada, el compose usa su propio otel-collector y Argus no entra en juego.

# ---- SEC-005: who may call the harness -------------------------------------------------------------
# Every Go service verifies a bearer token against a caller bundle and refuses to start without one.
# The bundle holds SHA-256 hashes, never tokens: it is config-as-code, which means committed, diffed
# and pasted into issues.
#
# The defaults point at examples/deep-research/callers.yaml, whose tokens are PUBLIC because they are
# committed — fine on a laptop, not fine anywhere another person can reach the ports. For a pilot:
#
#   token=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
#   printf '%s' "$token" | shasum -a 256     # the hash goes in YOUR bundle's tokenSHA256
#
# AEON_CALLERS_PATH=/examples/my-deployment/callers.yaml
#
# The worker's own credential. It is a caller, not a verifier — it never sees the bundle. Its entry
# declares mayApprove: false, so this token cannot approve the irreversible call the worker is blocked
# on; approving needs a caller whose entry says mayApprove: true.
#
# SEC-006: setting this here reached the three services that VERIFY tokens and never present one, and
# did NOT reach the worker — the only one that needs it, which stayed on the public committed
# development token. Measured. Both halves are fixed: --env-file makes this file reach the worker, and
# the three verifiers no longer receive the token at all. AEON_CALLER_TOKEN_FILE also works, which is
# the better path here specifically: the worker spawns the `claude` CLI with its whole environment.
# AEON_CALLER_TOKEN=

# ---- VRT-AEON-005 T-5: one Cedar bundle per tenant -----------------------------------------------
# AEON_POLICY_BUNDLE_DIR holds <tenant>.yaml, one per tenant, and the file NAME is the tenant. A
# caller whose tenant has no bundle is DENIED — a missing bundle is never a fallback to another
# tenant's permits, which is the failure this shape exists to remove (A2A-002 and RUN-006 each
# measured a resource landing inside a permit nobody wrote for it).
#
# Exactly one of DIR and PATH may be set; both is refused, for the same reason as `<NAME>_FILE`
# beside a plain value — a change applied to the one that loses has no effect and nothing fails.
#
# AEON_POLICY_BUNDLE_PATH (one bundle) still works and now means something explicit: it serves the
# single tenant AEON_TENANT_ID names. The same file, the same policies — what changed is that "who
# do these apply to" used to be "whoever called".
# AEON_POLICY_BUNDLE_DIR=/examples/policies

# ---- TOOL-008: what repository.read may read ------------------------------------------------------
# WHATEVER THIS POINTS AT IS READABLE IN FULL by any agent the policy bundle permits repository.read.
# The compose default is the repository's `docs/` directory, deliberately narrow. Pointing it at a
# source checkout was the first version of this feature, and a single call returned `.env` — with the
# Prometheus client secret in it — to a caller holding the public development token.
#
# The gateway refuses to start when the root contains files that look like credentials (.env, *.pem,
# id_rsa, ...), naming the file. That check catches the obvious cases; it does not make a wide root
# safe. The boundary is which directory you choose.
# AEON_REPOSITORY_ROOT=/repository
